N icmoptionratamnit  aedneznyimne idninvuolcvleod ine t(hNe cDyHto) ias mainc glycolysis pathway and respiratory cascade in mitochondria and therefore implicated in various diseases, such as cancer, Alzheimer’s disease, and diabetes.1,2 In living cells, NADH is oxidized to $\mathrm{NAD^{+}}$ in the electron transport chain of the tricarboxylic acid cycle in glycometabolism, donating its electron for ATP synthesis.3,4 In addition, NADH is also well-known for its reducing power as a redox cofactor and an up-regulator of the glutathione level.5 Therefore, endogenous NADH detection is beneficial to revealing its metabolic mechanism and to the diagnosis and treatment of related diseases. Currently, a variety of approaches have been established to detect NADH, such as electrochemical measurement,6,7 fluorescence detection,8 −10 and high-performance liquid chromatography.11,2 However, these methods often suffer from many limitations on the in situ monitoring of the endogenous NADH in living cells, such as cell destruction, expensive facility, vulnerability to various interferences, slow response, photobleaching, poor reproducibility, or poor specificity.
Surface-enhanced Raman scattering (SERS) spectroscopy is deemed a powerful tool for label-free detection of biomolecules with high specificity and sensitivity.12−17 More importantly, SERS shows many advantages in preventing photobleaching and autofluorescence, making it a potential technique for quantitative analysis and bioimaging in living cells.13−15 Generally, noble metal nanostructures (Au, Ag, Cu, etc.) can act as SERS substrates to greatly enhance the Raman signals even over 10 orders of magnitude via electromagnetic enhancement, which are highly suitable for the application of SERS in biomedical applications.16,17 For example, Masson’s group has fabricated SERS nanofibers decorated with plasmonic metal nanoparticles to monitor metabolites under different conditions.14,15 Specifically, when the SERS nanofiber was modified with a $\mathrm{\tt{pH}}$ -responsive molecule (4-mercaptobenzoic acid), it was able to linearly quantify intracellular pH over the range of 6.5−9.5.15 Recently, it has been reported that semiconducting metal organic frameworks (MOFs) can magnify Raman signals via chemical enhancement.18,19 Moreover, MOFs can also act as vehicles to carry individually dispersed SERS-responsive noble nanoparticles.18 Thus, MOF/noble nanoparticle hybrids can synergistically enhance SERS signals. Besides, MOFs have attracted increasing attention in colorimetric detection due to their intrinsic enzyme-like property.20 Therefore, we conceive that dualmode sensing systems based on MOF/noble nanoparticles may be designed where colorimetrics can provide convenient ensemble analysis and highly enhanced Raman signals can provide label-free and real-time detection at the cellular level.
Herein, a dual-mode biosensor, gold nanostar/ $\mathrm{CoFe}_{2}$ -MOF hybrids $\left({\bf A u}/{\bf C o F e}_{2}{\bf-M O F s}\right)$ , are devised to detect NADH based on either a colorimetric or SERS mechanism and reveal the dynamics of cell metabolism (Scheme 1). $\mathrm{CoFe}_{2}–\mathrm{MOF}$ serves not only as a peroxidase-mimicking nanozyme to catalyze the oxidation of $_{3,3^{\prime},5,5^{\prime}}$ -tetramethylbenzidine (TMB) dye molecules for the colorimetric detection of NADH but also as an enhancer for Raman signal and a carrier of Au nanostars (Au NSs) which present abundant sharp edges and tips to act as SERS-active “hot spots”. Such sensors exhibit high sensitivity and specificity toward NADH. Furthermore, it can reveal the intracellular distribution of NADH, its metabolismdependent dynamics, and the metabolic differences between normal and cancerous cells. The demonstrated strategy may be also applied to other key factors in the intracellular metabolic networks, whereby providing unique tools for both fundamental study of cell biology and diagnosis.
## EXPERIMENTAL SECTION
Synthesis of ${\mathsf{C o F e}}_{2}$ -MOFs Hybrids. $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $\mathrm{0.346\mmol},$ $93.4~\mathrm{mg},$ ), $\mathrm{CoCl}_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $\left(0.173\ \mathrm{\mmol}\right.$ , $41.2\ \mathrm{mg},$ , and ${\mathrm{NH}}_{2}{\mathrm{-BDC}}$ (0.519 mmol, $94.2~\mathrm{mg}$ ) are added into the DMF solution $\mathrm{^{\prime}16~m L}$ ) to dissolve by sonication for $10~\mathrm{min}$ . Then, the mixture is placed in the microwave reactor at $140~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $s\mathrm{~h~}$ .21 The red−black product is collected by centrifugation for $10~\mathrm{min}$ .
Synthesis of the Gold Nanostars $(\mathsf{A u N S s})$ . The surfactant-free method is used to prepare Au nanostars. Aqueous $\mathrm{HAuCl}_{4}$ solution $(20.0~\mu\mathrm{L},~10.0~\mathrm{mM})$ is first mixed with water $(1.0\ \mathrm{mL})$ ), and then $\mathrm{AgNO}_{3}$ solution $\left(3.0\mu\mathrm{L},10.0\ m\mathrm{M}\right)$ is added. After the solutions are thoroughly mixed, fresh ascorbic acid (AA) $(4.0~\mu\mathrm{L},~100~\mathrm{mM})$ is quickly added and vigorously shaken for $20\mathrm{~s~}$ at room temperature. After centrifugation at a speed of $4800~\mathrm{rpm}$ for $10~\mathrm{min}$ , the Au NSs are redispersed in water $(1.0~\mathrm{mL})$ ) for further use.22
Synthesis of ${\sf A u}/{\sf C o F e}_{2}$ -MOFs Hybrids. The prepared Au NSs solution $\left(20.0\ \mathrm{mL}\right)$ is used to disperse the synthetic $\mathrm{CoFe}_{2}$ -MOFs $\left(20.0~\mathrm{mg}\right)$ and mixed by sonication for about $60\mathrm{min}$ . The $\mathrm{{Au/CoFe}}_{2}.$ - MOFs are obtained by centrifugation.
NADH Detection by Colorimetric Method. First, NADH solutions with various concentrations $(50~\mu\mathrm{L},$ $1.93\times10^{-1}\:]$ M to 1.93 $\times10^{-8}\mathrm{M}$ ) are incubated with NAc-Ac buffe $\mathrm{~r~}\left(\mathrm{pH}4.5,2800\mu\mathrm{L},20.0\right.$ $\mathbf{m}\mathbf{M}\dot{}$ ). Subsequently, $\mathrm{Au}/\mathrm{CoFe}_{2}$ -MOFs solution $\left(1.0~\mathrm{mg/mL},50~\mu\mathrm{L}\right)$ , $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ ${}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\left(30\mathrm{mM},50\mu\mathrm{L}\right)$ , and TMB $\left(30\mathrm{mM},50\mu\mathrm{L}\right)$ are added into the above solution. Finally, the absorption spectra of mixed solutions are recorded after being incubated at $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $2\ \mathrm{min}$ .
SERS Measurement. The R6G is used as a Raman probe to estimate the SERS properties of $\mathbf{Au}/\mathbf{CoFe}_{2}–\mathbf{MOFs}$ . In a typical experiment, the $\mathrm{Au/CoFe}_{2}$ -MOFs $\left(1.0~\mathrm{mg}\right)$ are dispersed in $1~\mathrm{mL}$ of R6G solution $\left(10^{-6}\mathrm{~M}\right)$ over the course of $^\mathrm{~2~h~}$ for the SERS measurements. The SERS experiments are performed by Raman spectroscopy (inVia Qontor, Renishaw), and the excitation source is the $785\ \mathrm{nm}$ laser. The Enhanced Factor (EF) is calculated by the following equation:23
$$
\mathrm{EF}=\frac{I_{\mathrm{SERS}}}{I_{\mathrm{ref}}}\times\frac{C_{\mathrm{ref}}}{C_{\mathrm{SERS}}}
$$
where $I_{\mathrm{SERS}}$ and $I_{\mathrm{ref}}$ are the peak intensities of the R6G at $1510~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ on the SERS substrate and the reference substrate, respectively. CSERS and $C_{\mathrm{ref}}$ are the R6G concentrations on SERS substrates and reference substrates.
NADH Detection by SERS. NADH is examined by $\mathrm{Au/CoFe}_{2}$ - MOF substrates. First, the Raman probes are obtained by modifying 4-mercaptophenylboronic acid (MPBA) molecules on the surface of $\mathbf{Au}/\mathbf{CoFe}_{2}–\mathbf{MOFs}$ via $_\mathrm{Au}{-}S$ bonds. In brief, the mixture of MPBA $(200~\mu\mathrm{L},25~\mathrm{mM})$ in ethanol is added to the $\mathrm{Au/CoFe}_{2}$ -MOFs (0.8 mL, $1.0~\mathrm{mg/mL}$ ) solution and maintained overnight. After that, the solution is centrifuged at $8000~\mathrm{rpm}$ for $10~\mathrm{min}$ to remove the excess probe. Then, the obtained $\mathrm{\sfMPBA-Au/CoFe}_{2}–\mathrm{MOFs}$ are dispersed in water or culture medium for the following experiments. Different concentrations of NADH $(200~\mu\mathrm{L})$ are incubated with MPBA-Au/ ${\mathrm{CoFe}}_{2}–{\mathrm{MOFs}}$ $(800~\mu\mathrm{L})$ for $^{\textrm{1h}}$ . Then, the NADH-MPBA-Au/ $\mathrm{\dot{C}o F e}_{2}.$ MOFs are collected by centrifugation $\left(8000~\mathrm{rpm}\right)$ and dispersed by PBS $\mathrm{i}.0\ \mathrm{mL},$ $\mathrm{pH}~7.4\AA)$ ). NADH-MPBA-Au/ $\mathrm{\dot{C}o F e}_{2}$ -MOFs solutions $(10.0\mu\mathrm{L})$ at different concentrations are dropped onto aluminum foil for SERS measurements. For SERS studies, a $785~\mathrm{nm}$ laser is used. and the collective time was $3\mathrm{~s~}$ for a one-time measurement. SERS signals from random spots on the substrates are recorded to investigate the reproducibility. For in situ SERS imaging, MPBA$\mathrm{Au/CoFe}_{2}$ -MOFs solution (the final concentration was $10.0\ \mu\mathrm{g/mL})$ is cultured with HeLa cells or L929 cells $\mathrm{(1\times10^{5}~c e l l s/m L)}$ ). Then, HeLa cells or L929 cells are cleaned by PBS three times. After that, HeLa cells or L929 cells are treated with glucose ${\bf\Gamma}^{\prime}10.0~\mathrm{mM})$ for $^{\textrm{1h}}$ or pyruvic acid $\left(1.0~\mathrm{mM}\right)$ for $30~\mathrm{min}$ . The SERS imagings are recorded using the same conditions as above with extension of time.
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
Characterization of ${\sf A u}/{\sf C o F e}_{2}–{\sf M O F s}$ . The synthesis of $\mathrm{CoFe}_{2}$ -MOFs is schematically described in Figure 1A. First, amine-1,4-dicarboxybenzene $\left(\mathrm{NH}_{2}{\mathrm{-BDC}}\right)$ , $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , and ${\mathrm{CoCl}}_{2}{\cdot}6{\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}$ are mixed in $^{N,N}$ -dimethylformamide (DMF) and dissolved by ultrasound. The mixture is then placed in a specific tube and the tube kept in a microwave oven.21 The carboxyl groups of NH2−BDC could be coordinated with Fe3+ and $\mathrm{Co}^{2+}$ at $140~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to produce dark red $\mathrm{CoFe}_{2}–\mathrm{MOF}$ powder (Figure S1 inset). As revealed by scanning transmission electron microscopy (SEM) and transmission electron microscope (TEM), ${\mathrm{CoFe}}_{2}–\mathbf{MOFs}$ are octahedral with a relatively uniform size of ${\sim}200~\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure S1 and Figure 1B). The highresolution TEM (HRTEM) image is employed to determine the crystal structures of ${\mathrm{CoFe}}_{2}–\mathbf{MOFs}$ under the very low energy of an electron beam. As displayed in Figure 1C and D, the crystal planes belonging to [022] and [111] in $\mathrm{CoFe}_{2}.$ - MOFs are collected.24 The relevant fast Fourier transform (FFT) pattern of the ${\mathrm{CoFe}}_{2}–{\mathrm{MOFs}}$ is shown in Figure 1E. Subsequently, energy dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDS) on TEM is used to determine the element composition and distribution. As shown in Figure 1F, the distribution of C, O, N, Co, and Fe elements is homogeneous on the entire $\mathrm{CoFe}_{2}.$ - MOF particle, demonstrating that the proposed MOFs are bimetallic ${\mathrm{CoFe}}_{2}–{\mathrm{MOFs}}$ rather than simply mixing Co-MOFs with Fe-MOFs. Inductively coupled plasma mass spectrometry (ICP-MS) quantified the proportion of Fe elements and Co elements in the ${\mathrm{CoFe}}_{2}–{\mathrm{MOFs}}$ to be 3.27 and 1.48 wt $\%,$ respectively, which is almost equivalent to the feed ratio of $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $\mathrm{CoCl}_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (2:1).
Gold nanostars (Au NSs) are synthesized using a typical surfactant-free method and modified with thiol caproic acid via $_\mathrm{Au}-S$ bonds (Figure S2).22,25 $\mathrm{Au/CoFe}_{2}.$ MOF hybrids are prepared by mixing negatively charged carboxyl modified Au NSs with positively charged $\mathrm{CoFe}_{2}$ -MOFs via electrostatic interactions (Figure 1A). The octahedral morphology of $\mathrm{CoFe}_{2}$ -MOFs is clearly observed by TEM (Figure 1G), and Au NSs (around $60{-}90~\mathrm{nm}$ ) are randomly distributed on the surface of MOFs. The zeta potentials of Au NSs, ${\mathrm{CoFe}}_{2}–\mathbf{MOFs},$ and $\mathrm{Au/CoFe_{2}–M O F s}$ are $-32,+39.$ , and $+19\ \mathrm{mV_{\iti}}$ , respectively (Figure S3). Zeta potential changes further demonstrate that Au NSs are incorporated simply with $\mathrm{CoFe}_{2}$ -MOFs via electrostatic interactions.25 The crystal structures of $\mathrm{CoFe}_{2}.$ - MOFs and $\mathbf{Au}/\mathbf{CoFe}_{2}–\mathbf{MOFs}$ are further investigated by $\mathrm{x}$ -ray diffraction patterns (XRD) (Figure 1H). The main peaks of ${\mathrm{CoFe}}_{2}–\mathbf{MOFs}$ and $\mathrm{Au/CoFe_{2}\mathrm{-}M O F s}$ at $7{-}30^{\circ}$ are basically the same, ascribing to the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-101(Fe) typical signals.24,26 It also suggests that the crystal structure of ${\mathrm{CoFe}}_{2}–{\mathrm{MOFs}}$ is not destroyed after decoration with Au NSs. In addition, the XRD spectrum of $\mathrm{Au/CoFe_{2}\mathrm{-}M O F}$ powders displays two new characteristic peaks at $38.5^{\circ}$ and $44.1^{\circ}$ , consistent with Au [111] and Au [200].25 Furthermore, Fourier transform infrared spectra (FT-IR) of ${\mathrm{CoFe}}_{2}–{\mathrm{MOFs}}$ and $\mathrm{Au/CoFe}_{2}.$ - MOFs are similar (Figure S4). On the basis of the related results, it could be assumed that the structure of ${\mathrm{CoFe}}_{2}–{\mathrm{MOFs}}$ is not damaged after introducing Au NSs. Besides, X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) analysis also provides more evidence to confirm the successful synthesis of the as-prepared $\mathrm{Au/CoFe}_{2}$ -MOFs shown in Figure S5.
NADH Detection by Colorimetric Method. As shown in Figure 2A, $\mathrm{Au/CoFe}_{2}$ -MOFs possess excellent peroxidase-like activity. In the presence of $[\phantom{}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2},\mathrm{Au}/\mathrm{CoFe}_{2}$ -MOFs can oxidize colorless $_{3,3^{\prime},5,5^{\prime}}$ -tetramethylbenzidine (TMB) to blue color oxidized TMB (ox-TMB) with a characteristic peak located at $652\ \mathrm{nm},^{27}$ which is absent from TMB and $\mathrm{TMB}/\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ (Figure 2B). Like natural horseradish peroxidase (HRP), the peroxidase-like activity of $\mathbf{Au}/\mathbf{CoFe_{2}}\mathbf{-MOFs}$ is concentration and $\mathsf{p H}$ dependent (Figure S6 and Figure S7). According to the Michaelis−Menten, the Michaelis−Menten constant $\left(\Bar{K}_{m}\right)$ and the maximum reaction velocity $\left(V_{m}\right)$ are calculated (Figure S8). When TMB and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ serve as substrates, the $V_{m}$ values of the $\mathrm{Au/CoFe}_{2}$ -MOFs at $\mathrm{pH}4.5$ are around $8.1\times10^{-8}\mathrm{M}s^{-1}$ and $6.4\times10^{-8}\mathrm{~M~}s^{-1},$ , respectively. Meanwhile, when TMB and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ are introduced as substrates, the $K_{m}$ values are about $0.162~\mathrm{\mM}$ and $0.092\mu\mathrm{M}$ , respectively. $\mathbf{Au}/\mathbf{CoFe_{2}}\mathbf{-MOFs}$ possess lower $K_{m}$ and faster $V_{m}$ compared to $\mathrm{HRP},^{28}$ suggesting the strongest affinity and higher reaction activities.
·OH radicals can cause the oxidation of TMB to blue oxTMB, inducing the NADH detection by the colorimetric method; thus it is important to confirm whether ·OH radicals are generated by this nanozyme. Terephthalic acid (TA) can be oxidized by ·OH radicals to produce a fluorescence product with a maximum emission wavelength of $447~\mathrm{nm}$ .27 Compared to other groups, there is an obvious peak at $447~\mathrm{nm}$ for $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}/$ $\mathbf{Au}/\mathbf{CoFe_{2}}–\mathbf{MOFs}$ system, indicating ·OH is generated due to the higher catalytic activity of $\mathbf{Au}/\mathbf{CoFe}_{2}–\mathbf{MOFs}$ (Figure 2C). To further demonstrate the generation of ·OH, the Raman spectra of ox-TMB are recorded during the catalytic process shown in Figure S9. The results suggested that the high peroxidase-mimic activity of Au/ $\mathrm{CoFe}_{2}$ -MOFs can catalytically decompose $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ to produce ·OH, effectively oxidizing TMB to blue ox-TMB for further biosensor application based on the colorimetric method.
As a reductant, NADH suppresses the generation of blue oxTMB catalyzed by $\mathrm{Au/CoFe_{2}\mathrm{-}M O F s}$ (Figure 2A).29 Thereby, a simple and naked-eye colorimetric detection for NADH is realized. As shown in Figure 2D, the absorbance at $652~\mathrm{{nm}}$ decreased with increasing concentration of NADH. Consistently, visible color changes to colorless are observed as the concentration of NADH increases (Figure 2E inset). A good linear relationship between ${}_{\Delta}A$ $\left(_{\Delta}A\ =\ A_{0}\ -\ A$ , $A_{0}$ is the absorbance at $652~\mathrm{{nm}}$ before adding NADH shown in Figure S10) and the concentration of NADH from $19.3~\mathrm{nM}$ to 193.0 mM has been found in Figure 2E. The error bars indicate the standard deviation. In our experiment (Figure 2E), each point represents the mean and standard deviation of the absorbance of ox-TMB from 3 parallel measurements after adding NADH with a defined concentration. The linearly fitted line is ${}_{\Delta}A={}$ 0.01 ln $C\mathrm{~+~}0.77$ with an $R$ value of 0.987. The limit of detection (LOD) is $2.7\times10^{-9}\mathrm{M}$ . Based on the above results, the colorimetric method shows an excellent capability for convenient and low-cost analysis of NADH without the need of complicated equipment. However, an acidic environment is required by the nanozymes-based colorimetric method, and this limits NADH dynamics study in living cells.
NADH Sensing by SERS. Au NSs can enhance the SERS signals by electromagnetic enhancement (EM),16 and $\mathrm{CoFe}_{2}.$ - MOFs have semiconducting-like properties, providing the chemical enhancement (CM) of Raman signals.17 Due to the coexistence with the EM and CM, $\mathrm{Au/CoFe_{2}\mathrm{-}M O F}$ hybrids are excellent candidates for the detection of NADH (Figure 3A). In this work, the SERS enhancement capacity on different substrates is determined by R6G molecule, and the Raman spectra of R6G $\left(1.0\times10^{-6}\mathrm{M}\right)$ are recorded from MOFs, Au NSs, and $\mathbf{Au}/\mathbf{CoFe}_{2}–\mathbf{MOFs}$ under $785\ \mathrm{nm}$ laser irradiation, respectively (Figure 3B). Obviously, R6G bands are very weak on the aluminum even with higher concentration $(1.0\times10^{-1}$ M), whereas the peak intensities of R6G are noticeable on ${\mathrm{CoFe}}_{2}–{\mathrm{MOFs}}$ and Au NSs substrates, attributing to the greater SERS enhancement via CM and EM, respectively. Interestingly, with the decoration of Au NSs on ${\mathrm{CoFe}}_{2}{\mathrm{-MOFs}}$ , the highest peak intensity of R6G can be observed, attributed to the synergistic effect of the EM from Au NSs (called “hotspot”)21 and the CM from the ${\mathrm{CoFe}}_{2}–{\mathrm{MOFs}}$ . The average enhancement factors (EF) of Au NSs, ${\mathrm{CoFe}}_{2}–{\mathrm{MOFs}}$ , and Au/ $\mathrm{CoFe}_{2}$ -MOFs on the aluminum foil are calculated to be about $4.4\times10^{6},$ , $3.2\times10^{6},$ , and $2.3\times10^{7}$ , respectively (Figure 3C).23,30 Based on the above results, $\mathrm{Au}/\mathrm{CoFe}_{2}–\mathrm{MOFs}$ are employed for NADH detection on the basis of the SERS technique. Generally, target NADH is specifically captured by the boronic acid groups in 4-MPBA on Au/CoFe2-MOFs.9,31
After unwanted species are washed away, the captured NADH is then subjected to SERS detection.
To acquire more sensitive SERS signals, the optimized experiments for some parameters are conducted. In this work, the wavelength of the laser, the power of the laser, the concentration of MPBA, and the effect of the incubation time of NADH are the main factors influencing the sensitivity. As shown in Figure S11, the three strong Raman bands (1586 $\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , $1076~\mathrm{{\bar{cm}}^{-1}}$ , and $589~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ ) can be collected under three wavelengths of laser, attributed to $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C}}={\mathrm{C}}$ stretching, breathing vibration modes of the aromatic nucleus, and $_\mathrm{B-OH}$ shear vibrations in the MPBA spectra, confirming that MPBA shows high SERS activity.32 The following experiments are performed with a $785~\mathrm{nm}$ laser in order to gain a higher Raman scattering intensity at $1076~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ compared with a 532 or $633~\mathrm{nm}$ laser, and the Raman intensity of MPBA at $1076~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ is increased with increasing power of the $785\mathrm{nm}$ laser from $0.05\%$ to $100\%$ (Figure S12). The concentration of MPBA also has an influence on SERS signals (Figure S13). The characteristic Raman peaks at $1076~\mathrm{{\bar{cm}}^{-1}}$ gradually increase with increasing concentration of MPBA from $1.25~\mathrm{mM}$ to $5.0\ \mathrm{mM}$ and then plateau after $5.0~\mathrm{\mM}$ , suggesting a saturation of MPBA modification.
The optimum hybridization time is investigated by mixing NADH solution $\left(100.0\mu\mathrm{L},1.93\mathrm{mM}\right)$ with MPBA-Au/ $\mathrm{CoFe}_{2^{-}}$ MOFs solution $\left(1.0\mathrm{\mL}\right)$ and then incubated for different times (10, 20, 30, 40, 50, 60, 70, 80, and $90\ \mathrm{min}\dot{}$ ) at $4~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ When the NADH solution incubates with MPBA-Au/ $\mathrm{CoFe}_{2}.$ - MOFs, the Raman intensity at $1076~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ first increases and then remains constant after $60~\mathrm{\min}$ (Figure 3D). This phenomenon indicates that NADH molecules can specifically react to the boronic acid groups in MPBA, increasing the SERS intensity of MPBA under charge-transfer effects, and the action of orientation changes.33 Then the Raman intensity reached a platform after $60~\mathrm{{min}}$ , indicating complete hybridization. The intensity−time curve is shown in Figure 3E, which is in line with the result in Figure 3D.
The sensitivity is indispensable for evaluating the practicality of the established sensors. The concentration-dependent Raman measurements are evaluated on the $\mathrm{Au/CoFe}_{2}$ -MOF substrates. The Raman intensity at $1076~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ increases gradually with the increase of NADH concentration from 3.86 $\dot{\times}~10^{-4}~\mu\mathrm{M}$ to $3.86\times10^{3}\ \mu\mathrm{M}$ (Figure 3F). The plot of intensity $(I)$ versus the logarithm concentration $(3.86\times10^{-4}$ $\mu\mathbf{M}$ to $3.86~\mu\mathrm{M})$ of NADH shows that the relative SERS intensity linearly increases depending on the concentration (Figure 3G). The linearly fitted line is $I=1029\ln C+11314$ with an $R^{2}$ value of 0.979. Notably, as the concentration of NADH rises to $38.6~\mu\mathrm{M},$ the Raman intensity shows a sharp increase. The limit of detection (LOD) can be achieved to 2.8 $\times~10^{-11}$ M (the calculation process is shown in Supporting Information), which is much lower than that of the most reported methods depicted in Table S1. To evaluate the reproducibility of the present sensor, the reproducibility is first determined by repeatedly detecting NADH molecules (1.93 $\mathbf{m}\mathbf{M})$ on $\mathbf{Au}/\mathbf{CoFe_{2}}\mathbf{-MOFs}$ substrate at different spots on the same batch (Figure S14), and the SERS intensities at 1076 $\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ are averaged with five measurements. This method shows excellent reproducibility with a quite low value of relative standard deviation (RSD) of about $5.0\%$ . Similarly, the batchto-batch reproducibility is also acceptable with the RSD of $7.8\%$ . To evaluate the specificity of the proposed biosensor for NADH detection, several relevant interference species were selected for testing, including albumin from bovine serum (BSA), glucose (Glu), and glutathione (GSH). These molecules even at a much higher concentration than NADH (100 vs $2\mathrm{mM}$ ) did not cause significant interference with the detection BSA; Glu and GSH gave negligible SERS signal, whereas the SERS intensity increased rapidly after adding NADH, indicating that SERS can specifically detect NADH (Figure S15). The results demonstrate that the proposed sensor exhibits excellent specificity toward NADH.
NADH Dynamics in Living Cells. Inspired by the excellent performance of this NADH probe in vitro, the application of the proposed sensor for monitoring NADH dynamics is evaluated in live normal cells and human cancer cells. The cytotoxicity of $\mathbf{Au}/\mathbf{CoFe}_{2}–\mathbf{MOFs}$ is first assessed before the bioimaging analysis. Two different cell lines (fibroblast cells (L929) and human cervical cancer cells (HeLa)) are used to carry out the toxicity assay. Obviously, the $\mathrm{Au/CoFe}_{2}$ -MOFs showed negligible toxicity inside living cells even the concentration is as high as $200~\mu\mathrm{g/mL}$ (Figure S16). Therefore, $\mathbf{Au}/\mathbf{CoFe}_{2}–\mathbf{MOFs}$ are suitable candidates for bioimaging analysis of living cells. Besides, added materials further improved the negligible effect on the production of NADH in cells. Specifically, two cell groups at the same concentration $(5\times\mathrm{\bar{10^{5}}}\mathrm{cells/mL})$ ) with or without materials were cultured; the NADH was then extracted and quantified by the colorimetric method as described in the Experimental Section (Figure S17). Obviously, the material had a slight effect on the production of NADH, which was conducive to the in situ study of intracellular NADH concentration and distribution. Owing to the limitation of the in situ colorimetric method, SERS technique is used to investigate the metabolic NADH dynamics in living cells (Figure 4A). It is reported that intracellular NADH levels show an enhanced response during glucose metabolism.4 To demonstrate the capability of MPBA$\mathbf{\bar{A}u}/\mathbf{CoFe}_{2}–\mathbf{MOFs}$ for detecting intracellular NADH, living cells are treated with glucose $\mathbf{\Gamma}_{[10\mathrm{\mM}]}$ ). The SERS images of cells are collected using Raman Spectroscopy (Figure $\mathrm{4B-K})$ . As expected, when treated with $10~\mathrm{\mM}$ glucose, MPBA-Au/ ${\mathrm{CoFe}}_{2}–{\mathrm{MOFs}}$ incubated cells show a stronger Raman response at $1076~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ from the SERS mapping (Figure 4E and I) than in the absence of glucose (Figure 4C and G). Meanwhile, the Raman spectra of MPBA are measured in the living cells, upon pretreatment with glucose. Both L929 cells (Figure 4L) and HeLa cells (Figure 4M) exhibit an increase in the intensity at $1076~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ , which directly correlate with the increase in intracellular NADH levels. In the time-dependent study, the amount of NADH molecules increases within time from 2 to 6 h in either HeLa cells or L929 cells. The reason for this is that the relevant fluctuations of glucose levels have a significant effect on the NADH level, thereby resulting in a gradually increased NADH during glucose metabolism.34 Interestingly, the Raman intensity at $\bar{1076}~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ is larger in cancer cells (HeLa cells, Figure 4N) than that in normal cells (L929 cells, Figure 4N). The phenomenon is explained as followings:35,36 (1) The rapid proliferation of cancer cells demands more energy, giving rise to higher glucose consumption when compared to normal cells, which can cause higher NADH generation in cancer cells. (2) When glucose concentrations exceed physiological levels, altered metabolism can result from the high levels of glucose uptake and increased conversion of glucose via the glycolytic pathway (Warburg effect proposed by Nobel laureate Otto Warburg).36 In addition, overproduced pyruvic acid converts into lactic acid in the glycolysis pathway in cancer cells, which would accelerate the consumption of NADH.10 Therefore, MPBA-Au/ $\mathrm{CoFe}_{2}$ -MOFs as a SERS probe are used to determine the NADH level after adding pyruvic acid. As shown in Figure 4J,K, the Raman signal at $\mathrm{\hat{1076}~c m^{-1}}$ from the SERS mapping is weaker than that in the absence of pyruvic acid. The Raman intensity at $1076~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ also decreases, indicating the decrease in intracellular NADH levels after treatment with pyruvic acid (Figure 4O). These results demonstrate that the probe is a quite promising SERS tool for the determination of endogenous NADH and cellular imaging, which is consistent with previously reported work.9 To verify the accuracy of the Raman results, NADH is extracted from cells to quantify via the colorimetric method as shown in Figure S18. It could be seen that the absorbance of ox-TMB at $652~\mathrm{{nm}}$ decreases in cells after being treated with glucose, indicating more NADH generation after the addition of glucose. In contrast, the absorbance at $652\ \mathrm{nm}$ increases after being treated with pyruvic acid due to reduced NADH production. In addition, the absorbance of ox-TMB at $652~\mathrm{nm}$ is lower in HeLa cells than that in L929 cells, suggesting the higher content of NADH in cancer cells. The results are in line with the result of in situ SERS measurement. Therefore, the proposed dual-mode biosensor is not only capable of the facile and fast naked-eye detection of NADH but also a in situ supersensitive SERS probe for monitoring NADH dynamics in living cells.
## CONCLUSION
In summary, a new colorimetric and SERS dual-mode sensor based on $\mathbf{Au}/\mathbf{CoFe}_{2}–\mathbf{MOFs}$ is developed, which shows significant Raman enhancement and colorimetric response toward NADH. Based on the colorimetric mode, NADH can be detected with the naked eye in a linear range of $1.93\times10^{-8}$ to $1.93\times10^{-1}\mathrm{~M~}$ with the LOD of $2.7\times10^{-9}$ M. Such a sensor also shows a high sensitivity with a wide linear range of $3.86\times10^{-10}\mathrm{~M~}$ to $3.{\overset{\smile}{8}}6\times10^{-3}~]$ M based on SERS detection with the LOD of $2.8\times10^{-11}\mathrm{M}$ . Furthermore, the intracellular distribution of NADH and its dynamics during glycometabolism are revealed. This sensor represents a unique tool to reveal dynamic cellular processes involving NADH and identify the difference between cancerous cells and normal cells.
